The most common mistake is treating the parts as separate subjects. Students learn control theory and cannot apply it to a real motor, or wire a sensor and ignore its noise.

Other common errors are:
mixing units, for example millimetres and metres in the same equation;
forgetting that a real sensor has offset, drift and noise;
ignoring power supply and heat when choosing a component;
testing software only in simulation;
confusing steady-state accuracy with transient response;
skipping the safety check on a machine that can move.

Also state your assumptions. An examiner needs to see how you reached the answer, not only the answer itself.
